      "chapterNumber": 6,
      "headline": "The Crowded Mental Workbench",
      "keyInsight": "Most people think of memory as a massive storage locker. But your most precious mental tool is tiny: a small workbench called working memory. This little table can only hold three or four items before things start falling off the edge. If you try to carry a phone number while reading a road sign, your mental tray wobbles. Yet this tiny bottleneck is the secret engine of human intelligence. The master trick is not packing more raw items onto the table. It is bundling loose pebbles into neat packages. An expert cook does not track five separate ingredients. They track a single sauce. Master your mental workbench, and you master your focus.",
      "content": "Picture a chef working at a compact cutting board in a bustling food truck. On that little board, there is only room for a knife, a tomato, and an onion. If someone slides a fresh block of cheese onto the board, the onion rolls straight onto the floor. That tiny chopping board is your working memory. It is where you hold ideas while actively slicing, dicing, and combining them.\n\nYour brain keeps two small helper trays beside this main board. One tray is an inner voice that loops verbal sounds, like repeating a gate number at an airport. The other tray is an inner sketchpad that holds mental maps, like picturing how to steer your car through a tight garage. Because these two trays handle different senses, you can listen to a podcast while steering smoothly. But try listening to a podcast while reading an email, and your inner voice crashes.\n\nSitting above it all is the chief coordinator. This mental manager decides which task gets the spotlight and which distraction gets tossed out. When work gets frantic, this manager sweeps away the clutter to keep your main goal in view.",
