      "id": "cognitive-psychology-mind-and-brain-ch-7",
      "order": 7,
      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "The Air Traffic Controller Behind Your Forehead",
      "keyInsight": "We love to believe we are masters of juggling six tasks at once. In reality, your brain cannot run two conscious plans at the exact same split second. What feels like multitasking is actually high-speed switching. Imagine a busy restaurant kitchen with only one head chef. Every time an order changes, the chef drops the pan, wipes the board, and resets. That reset creates a hidden tax called switching cost. You burn huge amounts of mental fuel just reloading the rules for each task. The ultimate paradox of mental control is that trying to force everything forward at once leaves you moving slower than if you tackled one single dish at a time.",
